# Chapter 12 — The Scene

Up to now you have handled things one at a time — spawn a hero, move a ball,
check one coin against one wall. But a game screen is not one thing. It is a
*crowd*: a floor of tiles, a row of coins, a scatter of enemies, all present at
once. The arrangement of all of them together is the **scene**, and this chapter
is about building and counting a whole world in a few lines.

The key tool is a way to *count* the world — because once you can ask "how many
things are here, and of what kind?", you can build a level, check it, and tear it
down with confidence.

## An empty world

Before you place anything, the world holds nothing. `world/count` tells you how
many things are alive in it, and at the start that is zero:

```motoi
(require 'game/world)
(display (world/count))
(newline)
```

```
0
```

An empty stage. `world/count` with no argument is the whole census — every living
thing in the world — and right now there is nobody to count. This is your tape
measure for the scene: any time you wonder how full the world is, you ask it.

## Place a few things

Now populate it. Each `world/spawn` adds one thing, and the census grows to
match:

```motoi
(require 'game/world)
(world/spawn 'coin 10 10)
(world/spawn 'coin 30 10)
(world/spawn 'hero 50 50)
(display (world/count))
(newline)
```

```
3
```

Three spawns, three living things — two coins and a hero — so `world/count` is
`3`. This is scene-building at its most basic: name a kind, give it a spot, and it
joins the world. A whole level is really just many of these, arranged with care.

## Count one kind

A census of *everything* is useful, but usually you want a narrower question:
*how many coins are left?* Give `world/count` a kind and it counts only those:

```motoi
(require 'game/world)
(world/spawn 'coin 10 10)
(world/spawn 'coin 30 10)
(world/spawn 'hero 50 50)
(display (world/count 'coin))
(newline)
```

```
2
```

Same three things in the world, but asking `(world/count 'coin)` counts only the
coins — `2`. This is how a game knows when a level is won: spawn ten coins, and
each frame check whether `(world/count 'coin)` has reached `0`. Counting by kind
turns the census into game logic.

## Build a whole grid at once

Placing tiles one by one would be endless. `scene/grid` lays down a whole
rectangle of them in a single call — give it a kind, how many columns and rows,
and the size of each cell:

```motoi
(require 'game/world)
(scene/grid 'wall 4 3 16 16 0 0)
(display (world/count 'wall))
(newline)
```

```
12
```

One line placed a `4`-by-`3` block of wall tiles — `12` of them, each `16` pixels
square, laid out in a neat grid from the corner. This is how you build a floor, a
platform, a maze: not tile by tile, but a whole shape at once. The census
confirms all `12` arrived. Reach for `scene/grid` any time a level needs a block
of the same thing.

## Clear the stage

When a level ends, you want the world wiped clean before the next one loads.
`world/reset!` empties everything at once — spawns, grids, and all:

```motoi
(require 'game/world)
(world/spawn 'coin 10 10)
(scene/grid 'wall 4 3 16 16 0 0)
(world/reset!)
(display (world/count))
(newline)
```

```
0
```

You spawned a coin and a whole grid of walls, then `world/reset!` swept them all
away, and the census is back to `0`. This is the clean slate between levels: no
leftover walls from the last stage, no ghost coins. Build, play, reset, build the
next — that loop is the spine of a game with more than one screen.

## What you just proved

You learned to see the world as a crowd and to take its census. You counted an
empty world (`0`), filled it with three things, narrowed the count to just the
coins (`2`), built a twelve-tile grid in one line, and reset the whole stage back
to empty. Every program ran and reported a real count you could check by adding up
the spawns yourself.

You now have every piece a game needs and the means to arrange them by the dozen.
The next chapter is the payoff the whole book has been building toward: putting the
screen, the tick, the buttons, the collision, and the scene together into a small,
complete, *playable* game. Everything you have learned, in one program.

> **Tip**
>
> Let `world/count` be your win-and-lose detector. *No coins left* is
> `(= (world/count 'coin) 0)`; *no lives left* is `(= (world/count 'life) 0)`.
> Building your end conditions out of the census keeps them honest — they read the
> real world instead of a separate scorekeeping variable that can drift out of
> sync with what is actually on screen.

> **Warning**
>
> `world/reset!` is total and immediate — it removes *everything*, including your
> hero and any handles you were holding. After a reset, an old handle points at a
> thing that no longer lives in the world, and asking `entity/x` on it will not
> tell you anything useful. Reset, then re-spawn and re-bind fresh; never carry a
> handle across a `world/reset!`.

